[43.358049, "o", "**Verification run (all passed):**\r\n- `test -f /app/hello.txt` → regular file exists\r\n- `test -r` → readable\r\n- `grep -qx 'Hello, world!'` → that exact line is present\r\n- Python check on the decoded text → `strip()` equals `Hello, world!`\r\n\r\nOne detail worth flagging rather than burying: the file ends with a single newline (`od -c` shows `... d ! \\n`), which is the conventional POSIX text-file form and matches what `echo \"Hello, world!\" > hello.txt` produces. The visible text is exactly `Hello, world!`; if you need the byte-for-byte sequence with no terminator, that's a one-character change.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=5 surface=0b85225d90c7\r\n"]
